ty": 0.888}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "peixe", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fishes", "similarity": 0.862}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "peixe", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fish", "similarity": 0.888}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "fish", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fishes", "similarity": 0.979}, {"source_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "source_name": "fish", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "fish", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 8, "stable_key": "clu_fe7dbd2022f63834", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Fish", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "description": "Aquatic vertebrate animals with gills, fins, and typically scales.", "wikipedia_extract": "A fish is an aquatic, anamniotic, gill-bearing vertebrate animal with swimming fins and a tough cranium to protect the brain, but lacking limbs with digits. Fish can be grouped into the more basal jawless fish and the more common jawed fish, the latter including all living cartilaginous and bony fish, as well as the extinct placoderms and acanthodians. In a break from the long tradition of grouping all fish into a single class (Pisces), modern phylogenetics views fish as a paraphyletic group that includes all vertebrates except tetrapods. In English, the plural of \"fish\" is fish when referring to individuals and fishes when referring to species. Most fish are cold-blooded, their body temperature varying with the surrounding water, though some large, active swimmers like the white shark and tuna can maintain a higher core temperature. Many fish can communicate acoustically with each other, such as during courtship displays."}}, {"canonical_name": "grego", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "COUNTRY", "book_count": 9, "total_mentions": 44, "members": [{"entity_