", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Leaf", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "folhas", "similarity": 0.917}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Leaf", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "foliage", "similarity": 0.845}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "folhas", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Folio", "similarity": 0.841}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "folhas", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Foglie", "similarity": 0.884}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "folhas", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "folhas do feto", "similarity": 0.813}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "folhas", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "foliage", "similarity": 0.882}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Folio", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "foliage", "similarity": 0.939}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Foglie", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "foliage", "similarity": 0.954}], "id": 51, "stable_key": "clu_14f3b928e73ed1c0", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Leaf", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "description": "The part of a plant attached to a stem or branch.", "wikipedia_extract": "A leaf (pl.: leaves) is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata (pores that intake and output gases), the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}}, {"canonical_name": "Vomitos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 190, "members": [{"entity_id": "vomitos", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Vomitos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 161, "variants": ["Vomitos", "Vomitos continuos", "Vomitos de colera negra", "Vomitos de humor negro", "Vomitos de leyte", "Vomitos de leyte nas crianças", "Vomitos quotidianos", "Vomitos quotidianos colericos", "Vomitos verdes nas crianças", "Vomitosdehumor verde", "Vomitus", "vomita", "vomitar", "vomitaſſe", "vomiting", "vomitione", "vomitos", "vomitos continuos", "vomitos de agulhas", "vomitos de alfinetes", "vomitos de cabellos", "vomitos de cabeças depregos", "vomitos de colera com o comer", "vomitos de colera pura", "vomitos de coleras", "vomitos de cor negra", "vomitos de fangue", "vomitos de galinha", "vomitos de humor negro", "vomitos rebeldes", "vomitosconti", "vomits", "vomitum", "vomituras", "vomitus"], "contexts": ["Therapeutic practice discussed", "act of expelling stomach contents"]}, {"entity_id": "vomitings", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Vomitings", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 22, "variants": ["Vomitings", "Vomi", "Vomiting", "Vomi∣ting", "Vomi∣tings"], "contexts": ["symptom caused by fresh bark", "condition stopped by Bistort"]}, {"entity_id": "vomilo", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "vomilo", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["vomilo"], "contexts": ["illness entering hospital", "disease affecting coastal inhabitants"]}, {"entity_id": "vomitos", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "vomitos", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["vomitos"], "contexts": ["symptom of illness", "expulsion of stomach contents"]}, {"entity_id": "vomito", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "vomito", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "SYMPTOM", "count": 1, "variants": ["vomito"], "contexts": ["symptom of illness"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Vomitos", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Vomitings", "similarity": 0.972}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Vomitos", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "vomilo", "similarity": 0.929}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Vomitos", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "vomitos", "similarity": 0.968}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Vomitos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "vomito", "similarity": 0.814}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Vomitings", "target_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "target_name": "vomilo", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Vomitings", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "vomitos", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "vomilo", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "vomitos", "similarity": 0.963}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "vomilo", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "vomito", "similarity": 0.833}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "vomitos", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "vomito", "similarity": 0.848}], "id": 52, "stable_key": "clu_1050146bb34cd63b", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Vomiting", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "The act of expelling stomach contents; a symptom or illness.", "note": "Can refer to the symptom or a specific illness characterized by vomiting.", "wikipedia_extract": "Vomiting (scientifically known as emesis, and otherwise known as puking, and throwing up) is the forceful expulsion of the contents of one's stomach through the mouth and sometimes the nose. The substance so produced (the erstwhile contents of the stomach, now expelled) is also known as vomit or vomitus. Vomiting can be the result of ailments like food poisoning, gastroenteritis, pregnancy, motion sickness, or hangover; or it can be an after effect of diseases such as brain tumors, elevated intracranial pressure, or overexposure to ionizing radiation. The feeling that one is about to vomit is called nausea; it often precedes, but does not always lead to vomiting. Impairment due to alcohol or anesthesia can cause inhalation of vomit. In severe cases, where dehydration develops, intravenous fluid may be required."}}, {"canonical_name": "Pleurizes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 164, "members": [{"entity_id": "pleurizes", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Pleurizes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 159, "variants": ["Pleurizes", "Pleurigidem", "Pleuris", "Pleurisy", "Pleurit.", "Pleuritici", "Pleuritid", "Pleuritide", "Pleuritis", "Pleuritis ficca", "Pleuriz", "Pleuriz rebelde", "Pleurizes colericos", "Pleurizmuy", "Pleuriſes", "pleurisi", "pleurisy", "pleuriti", "pleuritide", "pleuriz", "pleurizes"], "contexts": ["chapter title about pleurisies", "inflammation of pleura"]}, {"entity_id": "pleurisy", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Pleurisy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 2, "variants": ["Pleurisy", "Pleuresy"], "contexts": ["acute illness", "inflammation of the pleura"]}, {"entity_id": "pleurisy", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "pleurisy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["pleurisy"], "contexts": ["acute inflammation of pleura"]}, {"entity_id": "pleurisy", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Pleurisy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Pleurisy"], "contexts": ["inflammation of pleura"]}, {"entity_id": "pleurisy", "book_id": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "name": "pleurisy", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "ACUTE", "count": 1, "variants": ["pleurisy"], "contexts": ["inflammation of the pleura"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Pleurizes", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Pleurisy", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Pleurizes", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "pleurisy", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Pleurizes", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Pleurisy", "similarity": 0.94}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Pleurizes", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pleurisy", "similarity": 0.933}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pleurisy", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "pleurisy", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pleurisy", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Pleurisy", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Pleurisy", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pleurisy", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "pleurisy", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Pleurisy", "similarity": 0.989}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "pleurisy", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pleurisy", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Pleurisy", "target_book": "first_principles_spencer_1862", "target_name": "pleurisy", "similarity": 0.989}], "id": 53, "stable_key": "clu_35ce3ed7307d574e", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Pleurisy", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "Inflammation of the pleura, causing sharp chest pain.", "wikipedia_extract": "Pleurisy, also known as pleuritis, is inflammation of the membranes that surround the lungs and line the chest cavity (pleurae). This can result in a sharp chest pain while breathing. Occasionally the pain may be a constant dull ache. Other symptoms may include shortness of breath, cough, fever, and weight loss, depending on the underlying cause. Pleurisy can be caused by a variety of conditions, including viral or bacterial infections, autoimmune disorders, and pulmonary embolism. The most common cause is a viral infection."}}, {"canonical_name": "Wounds", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 138, "members": [{"entity_id": "wounds", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Wounds", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 80, "variants": ["Wounds", "Wound", "Wounds in the Breast", "Wounds of the Sinews", "wounds"], "contexts": ["injuries to the body", "inward wounds healed by the herb"]}, {"entity_id": "wounds", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "wounds", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["wounds"], "contexts": ["injuries healed by ointments and nature"]}, {"entity_id": "green_wounds", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "green Wounds", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 19, "variants": ["green Wounds", "green Wound", "green and fresh Wounds", "green wounds"], "contexts": ["wounds healed by the herb", "fresh injuries"]}, {"entity_id": "inward_wounds", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "inward Wounds", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["inward Wounds", "inward Ulcers"], "contexts": ["injuries to internal body parts", "sores within the body"]}, {"entity_id": "bleeding_wounds", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "bleeding Wounds", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["bleeding Wounds"], "contexts": ["fresh, where powder is strewed", "injuries with blood loss"]}, {"entity_id": "wounds", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "wounds", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["wounds"], "contexts": ["injury to the body"]}, {"entity_id": "contusões", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "contusões", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["contusões"], "contexts": ["bruises", "condition treated by negundo"]}, {"entity_id": "feridas", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "feridas", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 31, "variants": ["feridas", "ferida", "ferida grande", "feridas da cabeça"], "contexts": ["healed by antimony", "wounds"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wounds", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "wounds", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wounds", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "wounds", "similarity": 0.984}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wounds", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "contusões", "similarity": 0.908}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Wounds", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "feridas", "similarity": 0.936}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "wounds", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "green Wounds", "similarity": 0.841}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "wounds", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "inward Wounds", "similarity": 0.899}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "wounds", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "bleeding Wounds", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "wounds", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "wounds", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "wounds", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "contusões", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "wounds", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "feridas", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "green Wounds", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "wounds", "similarity": 0.841}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "inward Wounds", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "wounds", "similarity": 0.899}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "inward Wounds", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "feridas", "similarity": 0.856}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "bleeding Wounds", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "wounds", "similarity": 0.904}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "bleeding Wounds", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "feridas", "similarity": 0.876}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "wounds", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "contusões", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "wounds", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "feridas", "similarity": 0.947}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "contusões", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "feridas", "similarity": 0.884}], "id": 54, "stable_key": "clu_a99fd74f79882061", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Wounds", "confidence": "high", "type": "disease", "description": "An injury to living tissue, typically involving a break in the skin.", "note": "Includes various types of wounds like 'green Wounds' and 'inward Wounds'. 'Contusões' (bruises) are also a form of wound.", "wikipedia_extract": "A wound is any disruption of or damage to living tissue, such as skin, mucous membranes, or organs. Wounds can either be the sudden result of direct trauma (mechanical, thermal, chemical), or can develop slowly over time due to underlying disease processes such as diabetes mellitus, venous/arterial insufficiency, or immunologic disease. Wounds can vary greatly in their appearance depending on wound location, injury mechanism, depth of injury, timing of onset (acute vs chronic), and wound sterility, among other factors. Treatment strategies for wounds will vary based on the classification of the wound, therefore it is essential that wounds be thoroughly evaluated by a healthcare professional for proper management. In normal physiology, all wounds will undergo a series of steps collectively known as the wound healing process, which include hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, and tissue remodeling. Age, tissue oxygenation, stress, underlying medical conditions, and certain medications are just a few of the many factors known to affect the rate of wound healing."}}, {"canonical_name": "brain", "category": "ANATOMY", "subcategory": "ORGAN", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 136, "members": [{"entity_id": "brain", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "brain", "category": "ANATOMY", "subcategory": "ORGAN", "count": 101, "variants": ["BRAIN", "brain", "Brain"], "contexts": ["physical basis for thoughts and feelings", "central organ of the nervous system"]}, {"entity_id": "brain", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "brain", "category": "ANATOMY", "subcategory": "ORGAN", "count": 3, "variants": ["brain"], "contexts": ["affected by drink spirits", "organ infected by poison"]}, {"entity_id": "brain", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "brain", "category": "ANATOMY", "subcategory": "ORGAN", "count": 1, "variants": ["brain"], "contexts": ["central organ of the nervous system"]}, {"entity_id": "brain-cell", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "brain-cell", "category": "ANATOMY", "subcategory": "TISSUE", "count": 5, "variants": ["brain-cell"], "contexts": ["individual unit of the brain", "cellular unit of the brain"]}, {"entity_id": "human_brain", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "human brain", "category": "ANATOMY", "subcategory": "BRAIN_REGION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Human Brain", "human brain"], "contexts": ["brain of a human", "brain of Homo sapiens"]}, {"entity_id": "brain-centres", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "brain-centres", "category": "ANATOMY", "subcategory": "BRAIN_REGION", "count": 2, "variants": ["brain-centres"], "contexts": ["specific areas of the brain", "areas in the brain supporting functions"]}, {"entity_id": "brains", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "brains", "category": "ANATOMY", "