"Escorpiao", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Scorpion", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ξ Scorpio", "similarity": 0.922}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Scorpions", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Scorpioni", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Scorpions", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "scorpion", "similarity": 0.821}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Scorpions", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Escorpiao", "similarity": 0.902}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Scorpions", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Scorpiomortiferum", "similarity": 0.825}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Scorpions", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ξ Scorpio", "similarity": 0.851}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Scorpioni", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Escorpiao", "similarity": 0.871}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Scorpioni", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Scorpiomortiferum", "similarity": 0.811}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Scorpioni", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "ξ Scorpio", "similarity": 0.899}], "id": 75, "stable_key": "clu_ce9780e6b6293711", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Scorpions", "confidence": "high", "type": "animal", "family": "Scorpiones", "description": "Arachnids known for their venomous sting, used in medicine and folklore.", "wikipedia_extract": "Scorpions (order Scorpiones) are predatory arachnids with eight legs, a pair of grasping pincers and a narrow, segmented tail, often carried in a characteristic forward curve over the back and always ending with a stinger. The evolutionary history of scorpions goes back 435 million years. They mainly live in deserts but have adapted to a wide range of environmental conditions, and can be found on all continents except Antarctica. There are over 2,500 described species, with 22 extant (living) families recognized to date. Their taxonomy is being revised to account for 21st-century genomic studies. Scorpions primarily prey on insects and other invertebrates, but some species hunt vertebrates."}}, {"canonical_name": "Berberi", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "berberi", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Berberi", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 8, "variants": ["Berberi", "BERBERI", "Bcrberi", "berberi"], "contexts": ["plant name, context incomplete", "common barberry"]}, {"entity_id": "barberry", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "Barberry", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Barberry", "barberry"], "contexts": ["genus with insect-assisted stamen spring", "flowers of"]}, {"entity_id": "beruena", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Beruena", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["Beruena", "Beruenz", "Berégenz"], "contexts": ["plant used for treatment", "plant from Spain"]}, {"entity_id": "berberis", "book_id": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "name": "Berberis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "PLANT", "count": 3, "variants": ["Berberis", "berberis"], "contexts": ["plant used for juice", "genus of plants yielding rusoif"]}, {"entity_id": "berberis", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "berberis", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["berberis"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a remedy for diabetes"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Berberi", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "Barberry", "similarity": 0.841}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Berberi", "target_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "target_name": "Beruena", "similarity": 0.856}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Berberi", "target_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "target_name": "Berberis", "similarity": 0.881}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Berberi", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "berberis", "similarity": 0.886}, {"source_book": "coloquios_da_orta_1563", "source_name": "Berberis", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "berberis", "similarity": 0.987}], "id": 76, "stable_key": "clu_0446aa6b8a21e523", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "barberry", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "linnaean": "Berberis vulgaris", "family": "Berberidaceae", "description": "Shrub with tart red berries, used in traditional medicine and as an ingredient.", "wikipedia_extract": "Berberis vulgaris, also known as common barberry, European barberry or simply barberry, is a shrub in the genus Berberis native to the Old World. It produces edible but sharply acidic berries, which people in many countries eat as a tart and refreshing fruit. It is a deciduous shrub growing up to 4 metres (13 feet) high. The leaves are small, oval, 2–5 centimetres (3⁄4–2 inches) long and 1–2 cm (1⁄2–3⁄4 in) broad, with a serrated margin; they are borne in clusters of 2–5 together, subtended by a three-branched spine 3–8 millimetres (1⁄8–5⁄16 in) long. The flowers are yellow, 4–6 mm (1⁄8–1⁄4 in) across, produced on 3–6 cm (1+1⁄8–2+3⁄8 in) long panicles in late spring. The fruit is an oblong red berry 7–10 mm (1⁄4–3⁄8 in) long and 3–5 mm (1⁄8–3⁄16 in) broad, ripening in late summer or autumn."}}, {"canonical_name": "Balſamo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 18, "members": [{"entity_id": "balſamo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Balſamo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 6, "variants": ["Balſamo", "Balíamo"], "contexts": ["precious liquid with marvelous effects", "imitation of true Balsamo"]}, {"entity_id": "baliamo", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "baliamo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["baliamo"], "contexts": ["balsam"]}, {"entity_id": "balsamo", "book_id": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "name": "Balsamo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 5, "variants": ["BALSAMO", "Balsamo"], "contexts": ["precious liquid from New Spain", "a miraculous liquor with medicinal virtues"]}, {"entity_id": "balsams", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Balsams", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "OTHER_SUBSTANCE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Balsams"], "contexts": ["medicinal preparations for wounds"]}, {"entity_id": "balſamo", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Balſamo", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 4, "variants": ["Balſamo", "balſamo"], "contexts": ["precious medicinal part of the liquor", "balsam"]}, {"entity_id": "balsoms", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Balsoms", "category": "SUBSTANCE", "subcategory": "PREPARATION", "count": 1, "variants": ["Balsoms"], "contexts": ["preparation made with moonwort"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Balſamo", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "baliamo", "similarity": 0.928}, {"source_book": "historia_medicinal_monardes_1574", "source_name": "Balsamo", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "Balsams", "similarity": 0.941}, {"source_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "source_name": "Balſamo", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Balsoms", "similarity": 0.865}], "id": 77, "stable_key": "clu_c4b1c7a801fc2ca4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Balsam", "confidence": "high", "type": "substance", "description": "A fragrant resinous substance obtained from certain trees, used medicinally.", "wikipedia_extract": "Balsam is the resinous exudate (or sap) which forms on certain kinds of trees and shrubs. Balsam (from Latin balsamum \"gum of the balsam tree,\" ultimately from a Semitic source such as Hebrew: בֹּשֶׂם, romanized: bośem, lit. 'spice, perfume') owes its name to the biblical Balm of Gilead. Balsams often contain benzoic or cinnamic acid or their esters. The Balsam of Matariyya was a substance famous as a panacea among physicians in the Middle East and Europe during the Antique and Medieval periods. The substance has long been used as a medicine, with early references to the substance recorded as far back as 285 BC. The Balsam of Matariyya was said to be derived from an Egyptian plant and is sometimes also referred to as the balm of Gilead or the balm of Mecca."}}, {"canonical_name": "mosses", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "mosses", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "mosses", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["mosses"], "contexts": ["plant type in ancient forests", "plant group with antheridia"]}, {"entity_id": "moss", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Moss", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Moss"], "contexts": ["small flowerless plants", "plant mentioned alongside Polypody"]}, {"entity_id": "moss", "book_id": "english_physician_1652", "name": "Moss", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Moss"], "contexts": ["description of Lungwort's growth", "fresh moss steeped in oil of roses"]}, {"entity_id": "moss", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "moss", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["moss"], "contexts": ["small, non-flowering plant"]}, {"entity_id": "mo'", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "mo'", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["mo'", "Moè"], "contexts": ["ingredient in a preparation", "moe, used in pills"]}, {"entity_id": "mochi", "book_id": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "name": "Mochi", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["Mochi"], "contexts": ["likely referring to a type of flour or meal", "ingredient in a plaster"]}, {"entity_id": "mosses", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "mosses", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 4, "variants": ["mosses"], "contexts": ["small, non-flowering plants", "low order of vegetable creation"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "mosses", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "Moss", "similarity": 0.926}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "mosses", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "mo'", "similarity": 0.851}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "mosses", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Mochi", "similarity": 0.847}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "mosses", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mosses", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Moss", "target_book": "english_physician_1652", "target_name": "Moss", "similarity": 0.9}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Moss", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "moss", "similarity": 0.983}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Moss", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "mo'", "similarity": 0.824}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Moss", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Mochi", "similarity": 0.82}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Moss", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mosses", "similarity": 0.926}, {"source_book": "english_physician_1652", "source_name": "Moss", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "moss", "similarity": 0.871}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "moss", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "mo'", "similarity": 0.854}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "moss", "target_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "target_name": "Mochi", "similarity": 0.832}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "moss", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mosses", "similarity": 0.934}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "mo'", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mosses", "similarity": 0.851}, {"source_book": "ricettario_fiorentino_1597", "source_name": "Mochi", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "mosses", "similarity": 0.847}], "id": 78, "stable_key": "clu_9dbe167e02e8454f", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "moss", "confidence": "high", "type": "plant", "note": "Early modern texts often referred to mosses collectively without specific taxonomic classification.", "wikidata_id": "Q25347", "wikidata_description": "division of plants", "wikipedia_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moss", "wikipedia_extract": "Mosses are small, non-vascular flowerless plants in the taxonomic division Bryophyta (, ) sensu stricto. Bryophyta (sensu lato, Schimp. 1879) may also refer to the parent group bryophytes, which comprise liverworts, mosses, and hornworts. Mosses typically form dense green clumps or mats, often in damp or shady locations. The individual plants are usually composed of simple leaves that are generally only one cell thick, attached to a stem that may be branched or unbranched and has only a limited role in conducting water and nutrients. Although some species have conducting tissues, these are generally poorly developed and structurally different from similar tissue found in vascular plants. Mosses do not have seeds and after fertilisation develop sporophytes with unbranched stalks topped with single capsules containing spores."}}, {"canonical_name": "bananes", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "bananes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "bananes", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 12, "variants": ["bananes"], "contexts": ["foodstuff", "fruit grown in the country"]}, {"entity_id": "beans", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Beans", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["Beans"], "contexts": ["food enjoined abstinence from"]}, {"entity_id": "beans", "book_id": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "name": "beans", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["beans"], "contexts": ["plant with seeds providing nutriment"]}, {"entity_id": "banana", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "banana", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 2, "variants": ["banana"], "contexts": ["plant family in tropical regions", "plant requiring hot climate"]}, {"entity_id": "banana", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "banana", "category": "PLANT", "subcategory": "OTHER_PLANT", "count": 1, "variants": ["banana"], "contexts": ["tropical fruit-bearing plant"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "bananes", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "Beans", "similarity": 0.856}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "bananes", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "beans", "similarity": 0.86}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "bananes", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "banana", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "bananes", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "banana", "similarity": 0.958}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Beans", "target_book": "origin_of_species_darwin_1859", "target_name": "beans", "similarity": 0.945}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "banana", "target_book": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "target_name": "banana", "similarity": 1.0}], "id": 79, "stable_key": "clu_4e2f73833f0dabb7", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Bananas", "confidence": "low", "type": "plant", "description": "Tropical fruit-bearing plants.", "note": "The mention of 'beans' in the Darwin context seems an outlier and likely a misattribution or a secondary example. The dominant context is 'bananes'.", "wikipedia_extract": "A banana is an elongated, edible fruit—botanically a berry—produced by several kinds of large treelike herbaceous flowering plants in the genus Musa. In some countries, cooking bananas are called plantains, distinguishing them from dessert bananas. The fruit is variable in size, color and firmness, but is usually elongated and curved, with soft flesh rich in starch covered with a peel, which may have a variety of colors when ripe. It grows upward in clusters near the top of the plant. Almost all modern edible seedless (parthenocarp) cultivated bananas come from two wild species – Musa acuminata and Musa balbisiana, or their hybrids. Musa species are native to tropical Indomalaya and Australia; they were probably domesticated in New Guinea."}}, {"canonical_name": "Milan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 17, "members": [{"entity_id": "milan", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "Milan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 10, "variants": ["Milan"], "contexts": ["city for temperature comparison", "city in Lombardy"]}, {"entity_id": "milan", "book_id": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "name": "Milan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Milan"], "contexts": ["city where Cardanus was a physician"]}, {"entity_id": "milano", "book_id": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "name": "Milano", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Milano"], "contexts": ["location of Buccola's research"]}, {"entity_id": "milan", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Milan", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 4, "variants": ["Milan"], "contexts": ["city where aërolite fell in 1660", "location of a monk struck by a stone"]}, {"entity_id": "milão", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "Milão", "category": "PLACE", "subcategory": "CITY", "count": 1, "variants": ["Milão"], "contexts": ["city where physicians were observed"]}], "edges": [{"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Milan", "target_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "target_name": "Milan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Milan", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Milano", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Milan", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Milan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "source_name": "Milan", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Milão", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Milan", "target_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "target_name": "Milano", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Milan", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Milan", "similarity": 1.0}, {"source_book": "pseudodoxia_epidemica_browne_1646", "source_name": "Milan", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Milão", "similarity": 0.944}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Milano", "target_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "target_name": "Milan", "similarity": 0.943}, {"source_book": "principles_of_psychology_james_1890", "source_name": "Milano", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Milão", "similarity": 0.915}, {"source_book": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "source_name": "Milan", "target_book": "polyanthea_medicinal", "target_name": "Milão", "similarity": 0.944}], "id": 80, "stable_key": "clu_4ef7f441d45255d4", "ground_truth": {"modern_name": "Milan", "confidence": "high", "type": "place", "description": "Major city in Lombardy, northern Italy.", "wikipedia_extract": "Milan is the regional capital of Lombardy, in northern Italy, and the seat of the Metropolitan City of Milan. It is the second-most populous city in Italy after Rome, with a population of 1.38 million in 2025. The city's wider metropolitan area is the largest in Italy, and the fourth-largest in the European Union, with an estimated population of 6.1 million. Milan is considered Italy's economic capital, and its metropolitan area accounts for about 20% of the country's GDP. Founded around 590 BC by a Celtic tribe, Milan was conquered by the Romans in 222 BC, who Latinized the name of the city into Mediolanum. At the end of the fourth century, it briefly served as capital of the Western Roman Empire."}}, {"canonical_name": "storms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "book_count": 5, "total_mentions": 15, "members": [{"entity_id": "storms", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "storms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 4, "variants": ["storms"], "contexts": ["geographical distribution of", "violent atmospheric disturbances"]}, {"entity_id": "tempêtes", "book_id": "relation_historique_humboldt_vol3_1825", "name": "tempêtes", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tempêtes"], "contexts": ["severe weather events"]}, {"entity_id": "storm", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "storm", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 3, "variants": ["storm"], "contexts": ["violent atmospheric disturbance", "weather phenomenon"]}, {"entity_id": "electric_storms", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "electric storms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 2, "variants": ["electric storms"], "contexts": ["storms with noise and light", "storms with electrical phenomena"]}, {"entity_id": "storms,_magnetic", "book_id": "kosmos_humboldt_1845", "name": "Storms, magnetic", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["Storms, magnetic"], "contexts": ["atmospheric disturbances"]}, {"entity_id": "tormenta", "book_id": "polyanthea_medicinal", "name": "tormenta", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["tormenta"], "contexts": ["metaphor for a difficult life situation"]}, {"entity_id": "storm", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "storm", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "variants": ["storm"], "contexts": ["violent atmospheric disturbance"]}, {"entity_id": "thunderstorms", "book_id": "connexion_physical_sciences_somerville_1858", "name": "thunderstorms", "category": "DISEASE", "subcategory": "OTHER_DISEASE", "count": 1, "varia